```markdown
# Satellite Structural Health Monitoring Using Piezoelectric Sensors and Machine Learning

A prototype Structural Health Monitoring (SHM) system for satellite structures using piezoelectric transducers, inertial sensors, Raspberry Pi, LoRa communication, Python-based data processing, and machine-learning-based anomaly detection.

The project focuses on collecting structural response data, transmitting the measurements wirelessly, and analysing the resulting data to identify abnormal structural behaviour.

---

## Overview

Structural Health Monitoring is an important part of maintaining the integrity and reliability of aerospace structures. Satellite structures are exposed to mechanical loads, vibration, thermal variations, launch conditions, and other environmental effects.

This project develops a prototype SHM architecture based primarily on piezoelectric transducers. The transducers are used to obtain electrical signals resulting from mechanical loading of the structure. An inertial sensor is used to monitor acceleration and vibration.

The acquired sensor data is converted into digital form, processed using a Raspberry Pi, and transmitted through a LoRa communication link. At the receiving end, an ESP8266-based system receives and decodes the data.

For offline analysis, Python is used to process structural datasets and investigate anomaly detection using Isolation Forest and Local Outlier Factor (LOF).

---

## Objectives

The main objectives of the project are:

- Develop a prototype structural health monitoring system for satellite structures.
- Use piezoelectric transducers to monitor structural response.
- Acquire vibration information using an inertial sensor.
- Convert analogue sensor signals into digital data.
- Transmit sensor data wirelessly using LoRa.
- Log acquired measurements for further analysis.
- Develop a Python-based data analysis workflow.
- Compare healthy and unhealthy structural datasets.
- Investigate machine-learning methods for anomaly detection.
- Visualise current, voltage, stress, strain, and anomaly-score data.

---

